WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.939
Three parcels slide across a bright paper
counter.

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:09.283
Each follows a different charging rule.
Their cost tags begin to move as

00:00:09.283 --> 00:00:10.106
quantities increase.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:20.987
The first tag stays at one height. More
parcels arrive, but the fixed fee does

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:24.189
not move. Its graph becomes a perfectly
horizontal ribbon.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:36.368
The second tag climbs by equal amounts for
equal quantity steps.

00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:42.606
A straight ribbon appears, carrying the
same slope all along its path.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.187
The third tag rises faster as more parcels
join.

00:00:49.187 --> 00:00:54.296
Equal quantity steps now add different
amounts of cost, and the ribbon bends

00:00:54.296 --> 00:00:54.689
upward.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:05.848
A new receipt combines a fixed fee, a
per-item charge, and a curved charge.

00:01:05.848 --> 00:01:09.189
Can we find its local rate without
starting again?

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:20.848
Choose a quantity on the receipt. What
happens to cost after a tiny increase?

00:01:20.848 --> 00:01:24.189
Find a rule that describes this changing
slope clearly.
